1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,720
The rank sum test is a hypothesis test that's normally attributed to the statistician Wilcoxon.

2
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:34,540
It is a two-sample test, and in that respect it's akin to the two-sample t-test that you will have met earlier.

3
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:55,980
The rank sum test is an example of a so-called non-parametric test, and we meet other examples of such tests, such as the signed test and the signed rank test.

4
00:00:55,980 --> 00:01:10,480
And the term non-parametric, we may recall, refers to the fact that the test makes no explicit assumptions about the nature of the distribution, or in this case distributions, which give rise to our data.

5
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:16,400
So, let us describe the test in general terms first.

6
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:39,180
So, suppose we have data from a random variable, we'll call it capital X, and let's sketch the density function of X, we'll assume that it's continuous, although we don't need to assume that, and it can be of any shape.

7
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:45,160
So that's the density function of X, and we'll denote it F subscript X.

8
00:01:45,160 --> 00:02:00,260
So, we have some data from this random variable, X1, X2, so independent observations, and let's say we have M of them.

9
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:13,720
Now, independent to that, we have data from another random variable, which we'll call Y, and its density function.

10
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:26,160
So, let's get to like that, and we have some data from Y.

11
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:38,900
Let's say we have N observations, N independent observations from the random variable, Y.

12
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:57,020
So, our null hypothesis, actually, is that the two distributions are exactly the same, so that the density function for Y is the same as the density function for X.

13
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:01,420
Whatever their shape, whatever they look like, they're the same.

14
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:21,340
Now, strictly speaking, the alternative hypothesis is that the density function for Y, let's put the argument in X there, is the same as the density function for X,

15
00:03:21,340 --> 00:03:28,400
but actually shifted by some constant, but actually shifted by some constant that will denote theta.

16
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:37,420
Now, in the null hypothesis case, actually, that's the special case of theta being zero.

17
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:54,220
So, the density function is not shifted, but we may have that theta is not zero, which leads to a two-sided case,

18
00:03:54,220 --> 00:04:09,600
or that it's greater than or less than zero, one or the other, which leads to one-sided cases.

19
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:13,940
So, let's clarify.

20
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:19,360
Let's go back to our random variable X and its density function.

21
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:27,340
The null hypothesis would state that, in fact, the density function of Y is exactly the same as the density function of X.

22
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:38,780
So, I could sketch over, the best I can do, the density function of X, and that would also be the density function of Y.

23
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:44,100
So, basically, both data sets come from exactly the same density function.

24
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:56,440
The alternative is that the density function of Y is exactly the same as that of X, but shifted by some parameter theta.

25
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:02,160
If I can draw it as best as I can do, so these density functions are supposed to be the same.

26
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:12,160
That's now the density function of Y, and it's just been shifted by some constant theta.

27
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:17,040
It could be shifted up or down, depending whether theta is positive or negative.

28
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:28,540
Now, this rather peculiar alternative hypothesis is sometimes overlooked

29
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:36,760
in textbooks that describe the rank sum test,

30
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:43,740
and often the test is taken as a general test for a change in location.

31
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:50,160
That is sometimes acceptable, except when we think about the test a little further,

32
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:58,840
the possibility may arise that simply testing for a shift in location

33
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:04,340
will not be satisfactorily detected by the test.

34
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:10,740
OK, so how do we do the test?

35
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:16,620
Well, we rank all the data,

36
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:20,800
that's all the X values and the Y values together,

37
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:22,920
in order,

38
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:29,240
smallest to largest.

39
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:34,280
And we'll give the smallest value rank 1,

40
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:38,300
and the largest value rank N plus M.

41
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:45,740
We find as our test statistic,

42
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:49,460
I'll call it W subscript X,

43
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:52,440
and it's the sum over the X values,

44
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:54,260
and remember we have M of those,

45
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:58,080
of the ranks of the X values.

46
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:00,740
So I'll denote by

47
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:03,380
R subscript I superscript X,

48
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:05,640
although we don't really need this notation,

49
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:06,860
because we won't use it again,

50
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:11,360
but it's the rank of the i-th X value

51
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:16,480
in the combined data.

52
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:19,120
So remember the smallest value

53
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:22,720
would have rank 1,

54
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:24,820
the largest value

55
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:30,640
rank N plus M,

56
00:07:31,380 --> 00:07:34,660
and I'll briefly describe what we might do

57
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:37,460
if we have ties at the end.

58
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:44,100
Worthwhile considering a special case,

59
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:47,500
supposing that the two data sets

60
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:49,240
were of the same size,

61
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:52,660
so we have as many X values as Y values,

62
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:56,580
then under the null hypothesis,

63
00:07:56,580 --> 00:08:01,080
well, the sum of the ranks of the X values

64
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:07,000
would have exactly the same distribution

65
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,620
as a random variable,

66
00:08:09,080 --> 00:08:10,220
we write it like this,

67
00:08:11,220 --> 00:08:14,140
as the sum of the ranks of the Y values.

68
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:17,120
They have exactly the same distribution.

69
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:19,780
There's no reason why WX

70
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:23,080
should behave any differently to WY

71
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:24,900
in the case where N equals M.

72
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:26,860
They will behave differently

73
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:29,100
if we have more X values and Y values,

74
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:29,980
or vice versa.

75
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:32,600
So that's a special case to have in our minds.

76
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:35,320
More generally, we ask the question,

77
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:36,960
as we always ask

78
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:38,640
when we're doing a hypothesis test,

79
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:40,580
is how does our test statistic,

80
00:08:40,580 --> 00:08:42,100
in this case,

81
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:44,060
how does WX behave

82
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:49,680
